{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nOn 2026-10-27 at about 17:36 PDT both `[remote \"origin\"]` and `[remote \"gerrit\"]` sections disappeared from the shared checkout's `/Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.git/config`. Every other section survived. After that, `make worktree` (the documented entry point for every change in `AGENTS.md`) and `git push gerrit` both failed. `make worktree` surfaced only git's raw `fatal: 'origin' does not appear to be a git repository`, which reads like a network or permissions fault rather than a missing config section.\n\nThe writer is identified in the 2026-10-27 comment on this ticket: a concurrent session made a `cp -R` copy of the linked `.tickets-tracker` worktree and ran `git remote remove` for each remote inside the copy. A copied linked worktree keeps the original's `.git` file, so `git -C <copy> rev-parse --git-common-dir` resolves to the real `/Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.git`, and the removal edited the shared config.\n\nReproduced in a sandbox on 2026-11-02: in a fresh repo with `origin` and `gerrit`, `cp -R` of a linked worktree followed by `git -C <copy> remote remove <r>` for each remote left `git remote -v` in the main checkout empty. Running the repository `Makefile`'s `make worktree name=x` there printed git's raw \"does not appear to be a git repository\" text before the recipe's generic `'git fetch origin' failed` line.\n\n## Approach\nMake the remote configuration recoverable from the tree and make `make worktree` diagnose a missing remote by name, before git's misleading text appears.\n\n1. **Record the canonical URLs in a tracked runbook.** Add a section \"Restoring the `origin` and `gerrit` remotes\" to `docs/local-dev-env.md` that lists `origin` = `https://github.com/navapbc/rebar.git` (already the clone URL in `docs/clone-guidance.md:6`) and `gerrit` = `https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/a/rebar` (the host and path used in `CONTRIBUTING.md:63`). It gives the exact `git remote add` restore commands and a `git fetch origin` check. It also notes that a contributor may embed their own Gerrit username per `CONTRIBUTING.md` §1b. The section says that a copied linked worktree shares the original's config, which explains how the remotes can disappear.\n2. **Preflight in `make worktree`.** In the `worktree` recipe (`Makefile:227`), before the existing `git fetch origin` step (`Makefile:249-252`), run `git remote get-url origin`. If it fails, print an `ERROR:` line naming the missing `origin` remote, print the `git remote add origin https://github.com/navapbc/rebar.git` restore command, point to the new runbook section, and exit non-zero **without** running `git fetch` or `git worktree add`. When `gerrit` is missing, print a non-fatal `WARNING:` naming the missing `gerrit` remote and the same runbook section, then continue, because creating a worktree does not need `gerrit` and only the later push does.\n3. **Guard against drift.** A unit test asserts that the URLs the Makefile prints are the same strings the runbook records.\n\nWhy this fits: the failure happens in the recipe that every change starts with, so the message has to come from there. Putting the URLs in tracked docs means recovery no longer depends on someone's terminal history. Preventing `cp -R` copies of linked worktrees from mutating shared state is a separate, broader defect class. It is filed as its own bug, linked `discovered_from` this one, and is not fixed here.\n\n## Scope\n- `Makefile`: `worktree` recipe preflight for `origin` (fatal) and `gerrit` (warning).\n- `docs/local-dev-env.md`: new \"Restoring the `origin` and `gerrit` remotes\" section, with a pointer from the existing \"Starting a NEW worktree?\" note.\n- `tests/unit/test_makefile_worktree.py`: extend the existing `_GIT_STUB` sandbox so `git remote get-url <name>` can fail for chosen remotes, and add tests.\n\nOut of scope: guarding `cp -R` of linked worktrees (separate bug), and any change to `git` configuration on the host.\n\n## Testing\nExtend `tests/unit/test_makefile_worktree.py`, which already runs the real `Makefile` against a stub `git` on `PATH`. Today `_GIT_STUB` exits 0 for every command other than `fetch` and `worktree add`, so the existing tests keep passing unchanged.\n- Missing `origin`: exit is non-zero, combined output has an `ERROR` line naming `origin`, includes `git remote add origin https://github.com/navapbc/rebar.git` and the runbook pointer, does not include git's \"does not appear to be a git repository\" text, and the stub log shows no `fetch` and no `worktree add`.\n- Missing `gerrit` only: exit 0, `✓ worktree ready` is printed, and a `WARNING` line names `gerrit` and the runbook.\n- Both present: the existing happy-path test still passes, and no `WARNING` about remotes is printed.\n- Drift: each URL that appears in the recipe's restore text also appears in `docs/local-dev-env.md`.\nWrite these tests first and confirm they fail against the current recipe.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The writer of the 17:36 `.git/config` rewrite is identified, or the investigation records exactly which sources were checked and found silent.\n- [ ] The canonical `origin` and `gerrit` URLs are recorded in a tracked file or runbook (`docs/local-dev-env.md`), so recovery does not depend on terminal history; verified by `grep -n \"navapbc/rebar.git\\|rebar.solutions.navateam.com/a/rebar\" docs/local-dev-env.md` returning both.\n- [ ] `make worktree` fails with a message naming the missing remote and pointing to the restore step, instead of surfacing git's raw \"does not appear to be a git repository\"; verified by the new tests in `tests/unit/test_makefile_worktree.py` passing under `pytest tests/unit/test_makefile_worktree.py`.\n- [ ] `make verify` exits 0."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793681115178961001,"uuid":"8931c817-488e-49d9-8edd-00ee1d728a8e"}